 McKee (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island's governor's race became an open-seat contest when Helena Foulkes defeated incumbent Dan McKee in September's Democratic primary, a rare defeat fueled partly by frustration over the stalled Washington Bridge project. She now faces Republican Aaron Guckian, who secured his party's nomination in mid-September, along with three independent candidates including Ken Block.\n\nThe state leans Democratic by eight points, and every recent poll shows Foulkes well ahead, leading Guckian by roughly 25 to 27 points in surveys from the University of New Hampshire and Emerson College. All six expert raters call the race solidly or safely Democratic, and prediction markets give Foulkes a 97 to 98 percent chance of winning. Her vulnerabilities are a sub-50 percent polling standing, a crowded independent field, and a report alleging improper campaign donations, but none has yet dented her lead.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 18.1}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.997, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9964, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.9941, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9953, "margin": 18.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9924, "margin": 19.0}]}